---
import ReferenceLayout from '../../components/ReferenceLayout.astro';

const contents = [
  { href: '#first-project', label: 'Create a project' },
  { href: '#project-files', label: 'Work with files' },
  { href: '#editor', label: 'Use the editor' },
  { href: '#git', label: 'Use Git' },
  { href: '#cmake', label: 'Configure CMake' },
  { href: '#build-run', label: 'Build and run' },
  { href: '#debugging', label: 'Debug a program' },
  { href: '#beta-limits', label: 'Check beta limits' },
];
---

<ReferenceLayout
  pageTitle="Docs | cppMax"
  metaDescription="Temporary user documentation for projects, editing, Git, CMake, building, and debugging in cppMax."
  heading="Use cppMax."
  introduction="A temporary guide to the current beta interface and project workflow."
  contents={contents}
>
  <p class="temporary-note">Temporary user guide. Screens, button names, and exact steps will be updated from the final beta interface.</p>

  <section id="first-project">
    <p class="section-label">Create a project</p>
    <h2>Start with a small C++ project.</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Open the project browser.</li>
      <li>Create a project and choose its local folder.</li>
      <li>Open <code>main.cpp</code> in the editor.</li>
      <li>Add a small program, then save the file.</li>
    </ol>
    <div class="code-block"><div><span>main.cpp</span><span>C++</span></div><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;

int main() &#123;
  std::cout &lt;&lt; "Hello from cppMax!\n";
  return 0;
&#125;</pre></div>
  </section>

  <section id="project-files">
    <p class="section-label">Work with files</p>
    <h2>Keep source, headers, and build files together.</h2>
    <p>Use the project tree to move through folders and open files. A project can contain several source and header files. Open files appear in tabs so you can switch between them.</p>
    <p>Use normal C++ file extensions and keep <code>CMakeLists.txt</code> at the appropriate project level when the project uses CMake.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="editor">
    <p class="section-label">Use the editor</p>
    <h2>Edit code and review basic diagnostics.</h2>
    <p>The beta editor provides C++ syntax highlighting and basic static analysis. Save changes before starting a build, then use the reported file and line information to find compiler errors.</p>
    <p>The editor and diagnostics are still basic. Deeper analysis and interface customization are planned for v1.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="git">
    <p class="section-label">Use Git</p>
    <h2>Clone a repository or track a local project.</h2>
    <p>Clone an existing repository from the project browser, or initialize Git for a local project. Review changes before creating a commit. Branch, pull, and push operations are available through the Git controls.</p>
    <p>Remote operations need a network connection. Local editing, commits, builds, and runs do not need a remote build machine.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="cmake">
    <p class="section-label">Configure CMake</p>
    <h2>Begin with a small configuration.</h2>
    <p>Most common CMake project setups work in the beta. Start with the project's main <code>CMakeLists.txt</code>, select the intended target, and run configuration before building.</p>
    <p>A project with platform-specific dependencies or unusual build scripts may need changes.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="build-run">
    <p class="section-label">Build and run</p>
    <h2>Build the selected target, then open the console.</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Select the project target.</li>
      <li>Start a build and review compiler diagnostics.</li>
      <li>Approve the signing step when the beta asks for it.</li>
      <li>Run the completed build.</li>
      <li>Read program output in the console.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>An unchanged signed build can run again. Rebuild after changing the program.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="debugging">
    <p class="section-label">Debug a program</p>
    <h2>Use breakpoints for the first debugging pass.</h2>
    <p>Add a breakpoint to an executable line, start the program in the debugger, and inspect where execution pauses. Remove or disable the breakpoint when it is no longer needed.</p>
    <p>Breakpoints are basic in the current beta. Advanced debugging tools are planned for v1.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="beta-limits">
    <p class="section-label">Check beta limits</p>
    <h2>Confirm support before moving a large project.</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Syntax highlighting, static analysis, and breakpoints are basic.</li>
      <li>Most CMake projects work, but platform-specific dependencies may not.</li>
      <li>The current runtime supports a focused system-call set.</li>
      <li>OpenGL is planned for v1 and is not available in the beta.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</ReferenceLayout>
